## Findings
First executed screen: full native backgrounds contain 31,010 gene symbols (GSE177037, 16 libraries) and 17,327 genes (GSE104324, six libraries; HTSeq special counters excluded, not treated as genes). All seven planned contrasts pass the fixed target/myelin7 baseline floor. Producer screen_selectivity.py completed with receipt screen-execution-r002.json; r001 failed an incorrect hard-coded sample-title assertion before writing outputs. Corrected sample mapping now uses exact asset filename/accession and GEO title, and correct treatment is 10 nM NRG1beta1, not the initially miscoded ng/ml string. Failed execution preserved.

Exploratory Pmp22-minus-myelin7 mean log2 changes: recovered O4-positive pools d3/d5/d7 -0.696/-0.954/-0.367; separate whole-nerve pools -1.331/-1.761/-1.315. Two libraries per arm, potentially overlapping donor pools, shared naive controls: these are not six independent replications and Welch intervals are conditional descriptive pool intervals, not population-level confidence. Exact conditional permutation p=1/3 for each. Target is not uniquely most suppressed: Mag/Prx/Mal can fall more. The day5 recovered-pool estimate remains below -0.5 under each marker omission; this motivates a falsifiable independent early-injury transfer test, not a causal selective mechanism claim.

NRG1 10 nM for 6 h in adult-rat primary cultured SC (three independent experiments stated in GEO, donor/split map unresolved) gives Pmp22 -1.572, myelin7 -0.913, relative -0.660; unpaired 95% interval [-1.615,+0.296], permutation p=0.2. Every marker omission keeps a point estimate below -0.5, but a leave-one-library estimate reaches -0.408. This is a gene-panel-relative lead, not inferentially established selectivity. The source paper already reports soluble NRG1 suppression of myelination; novelty must be limited to a checked relative program comparison.

